PART 2️⃣ Distributed Data Patterns

(The hardest part of microservices)

❌ Why SQL Joins Don’t Work

Each service has its own database:

Book Service → books DB

Order Service → orders DB

Payment Service → payments DB

You cannot do:

SELECT * FROM orders o JOIN books b ON ...

1️⃣ Saga Pattern (VERY IMPORTANT)
🔹 Problem

Order creation involves multiple services:

Create Order

Reduce Book Stock

Take Payment

What if step 2 fails?

🔹 Saga Pattern Solution
Two Types:
Type	Explanation
Choreography	Services talk via events
Orchestration	One service controls the flow

We’ll explain Orchestration (easier).

🔸 Example Saga Flow (Orchestration)
Order Service
 ├─ Create Order (PENDING)
 ├─ Call Book Service → Reduce Stock
 ├─ Call Payment Service → Deduct Money
 ├─ If all OK → Order CONFIRMED
 └─ If any fails → Rollback (COMPENSATION)

🔸 Simple Code Idea (Order Service)
public void placeOrder(Order order) {
    try {
        bookClient.reduceStock(order.getBookId());
        paymentClient.pay(order.getAmount());
        order.setStatus("CONFIRMED");
    } catch (Exception e) {
        // Compensation
        bookClient.restoreStock(order.getBookId());
        order.setStatus("CANCELLED");
    }
}


✅ This is a Saga with compensation.

2️⃣ CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)
🔹 Problem

Writes are heavy

Reads are heavy

Same DB for both causes performance issues

🔹 CQRS Solution

Separate:

Command Model → Writes

Query Model → Reads

🔸 Example
Write Service
POST /orders
PUT /orders/{id}


DB:

orders_write_db

Read Service
GET /orders
GET /orders/{id}


DB:

orders_read_db (optimized, denormalized)

🔸 How data syncs?

Events (Kafka / RabbitMQ)

Read DB updated asynchronously

🔸 When to use CQRS?

❌ Small apps → NO
✅ Large-scale, high traffic → YES

